content.text:
Fixed: in scoped CSS, a nested `@media` (or `@container`, or any other at-rule) now gets its bare declarations wrapped in `&[data-v-xxx]`, the same as the parent rule's own declarations.

**Cause:** in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`, when a scoped rule has nested child rules, its direct declarations get moved into a `& { … }` rule so the scope attribute can be attached. That step only looked at the rule's own declarations. Any declarations sitting directly inside a nested at-rule stayed unwrapped and unscoped, so they lost on specificity to the scoped `&[data-v-…]` declarations above them.

**Change:** I pulled the wrapping into a helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule and then on each of the rule's direct at-rule children.

With the issue's example plus a nested `.bar` inside the `@media`, the output is now:

```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background: green }
.bar[data-v-test] { color: x }
}
}
```

All 413 existing compiler-sfc tests pass. I checked that output with a temporary test file, which I've deleted; I didn't add a permanent test.

Two limits on the fix:
- Only at-rules that sit directly inside the rule are handled. An at-rule nested inside another at-rule (say `@supports` inside `@media`) still won't have its declarations wrapped.
- Comments next to those declarations aren't moved into the `&` rule, matching how the existing code treats the rule's own declarations.
